<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Epic · c. 1800 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Epic of Gilgamesh</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sux">Shūtur eli sharrī (&quot;Surpassing All Other Kings&quot;)</bdi> / <bdi lang="sux-Latn">Sha naqba īmuru (&quot;He Who Saw the Deep&quot;)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The oldest great story we know: a proud king of Uruk finds a true friend, loses him, and goes to the ends of the earth to learn why we must die.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (the Standard Babylonian version was compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (no music for it survives)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Babylonian and Assyrian Mesopotamia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian, Akkadian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Mesopotamia (present-day Iraq)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Old Babylonian version 18th century BCE; Standard Babylonian version between the 13th and 10th centuries BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Epic</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (an ancient text; the Sumerian lines printed here and their translation are those of the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford), licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>, from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, is the world's oldest surviving example of narrative literature. Its history begins with five Sumerian poems about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, some of which may date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> (c. 2100 BCE). These independent stories were later used as material for a combined epic in Akkadian. The first surviving version of it, the &quot;Old Babylonian&quot; version, dates to the 18th century BCE and is named after its opening words, Shūtur eli sharrī, &quot;Surpassing All Other Kings&quot;; only a few of its tablets survive. The later Standard Babylonian version, compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni between the 13th and 10th centuries BCE, opens Sha naqba īmuru, &quot;He Who Saw the Deep&quot;. About two thirds of its twelve tablets have been recovered, some of the best copies from the library of the 7th-century BCE Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><p>In the first half of the story <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, two-thirds god and one-third man, oppresses his people, and the gods create the wild man Enkidu as his equal. Tamed by Shamhat, Enkidu comes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and fights Gilgamesh; the two become friends, travel to the Cedar Forest, kill its guardian Humbaba and, when the goddess Ishtar sends the Bull of Heaven against them, kill the Bull too. The gods condemn Enkidu to die of an illness. In the second half, Gilgamesh, stricken by his friend's death and afraid of his own, journeys to the end of the world to find <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Utnapishtim</a>, the only man who survived the great flood with his wife and was given eternal life. He learns that immortality is not for him; a plant that restores youth is stolen by a serpent, and he returns to Uruk, whose great walls he praises.</p><p>About 15,000 fragments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets were found in the library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> and W. K. Loftus in the early 1850s. In 1872 George Smith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> read translated fragments, among them the flood story, before the Society of Biblical Archaeology, and he published fuller translations in 1875 and 1876. Andrew George's critical edition of 2003 is now the standard one. The performance on this page is sung in Sumerian, the language of the older poems: its opening, &quot;ud re-a ud su₃-ra₂ re-a&quot; (&quot;In those days, in those distant days&quot;), is the first line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, Enkidu and the Netherworld, a Sumerian poem of 330 lines that the twelfth tablet of the epic mainly translates into Akkadian. The words printed here are its prologue of 26 lines, on the beginnings of the world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a>'s voyage by boat to the netherworld; the story then turns to the huluppu tree that Gilgamesh fells for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The epic asks what a life is worth if it must end. Its hero begins as a tyrant, is changed by friendship, is broken by grief, and searches for a way to escape death, only to learn that the gods kept life for themselves and gave death to humankind. What remains to him are the things people make and share: a friend, a well-ruled city, the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and the story itself.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Sumerian (transliteration)</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ud re-a ud su₃-ra₂ re-a</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ĝi₆ re-a ĝi₆ ba₉-ra₂ re-a</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">mu re-a mu su₃-ra₂ re-a</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ud ul niĝ₂-du₇-e pa ed₂-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ud ul niĝ₂-du₇-e mi₂ zid dug₄-ga-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">eš₃ kalam-ma-ka ninda šu₂-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{im}šu-rin-na kalam-ma-ka niĝ₂-tab ak-a-ba</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">an ki-ta ba-da-ba₉-ra₂-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ki an-ta ba-da-sur-ra-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">mu nam-lu₂-u₁₈-lu ba-an-ĝar-ra-a-ba</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ud an-ne₂ an ba-an-de₆-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{d}en-lil₂-le ki ba-an-de₆-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{d}ereš-ki-gal-la-ra kur-ra saĝ rig₇-bi-še₃ im-ma-ab-rig₇-a-ba</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ba-u₅-a-ba ba-u₅-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">a-a kur-še₃ ba-u₅-a-ba</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{d}en-ki kur-še₃ ba-u₅-a-ba</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">lugal-ra tur-tur ba-an-da-ri</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{d}en-ki-ra gal-gal ba-an-da-ri</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">tur-tur-bi na₄ šu-kam</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">gal-gal-bi na₄ gi gu₄-ud-da-kam</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ur₂ {ĝiš}ma₂ tur-re {d}en-ki-ka₃-ke₄</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">niĝ₂-bun₂-na du₇-am₃ mi-šu₂-šu₂</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">lugal-ra a {ĝiš}ma₂ saĝ-ĝa₂-ke₄</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ur-bar-ra-gin₇ teš₂ mu-na-gu₇-e</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">{d}en-ki-ra a {ĝiš}ma₂ eĝer-ra-ke₄</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sux-Latn">ur-maḫ-gin₇ saĝ ĝiš im-ra-ra</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>In those days, in those distant days, in those nights, in those remote nights, in those years, in those distant years;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>in days of yore, when the necessary things had been brought into manifest existence, in days of yore, when the necessary things had been for the first time properly cared for, when bread had been tasted for the first time in the shrines of the Land, when the ovens of the Land had been made to work, when the heavens had been separated from the earth, when the earth had been delimited from the heavens, when the fame of mankind had been established, when An had taken the heavens for himself, when Enlil had taken the earth for himself, when the nether world had been given to Ereš-kigala as a gift;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>when he set sail, when he set sail, when the father set sail for the nether world, when Enki set sail for the nether world — against the king a storm of small hailstones arose, against Enki a storm of large hailstones arose.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The small ones were light hammers, the large ones were like stones from catapults (?).</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The keel of Enki's little boat was trembling as if it were being butted by turtles, the waves at the bow of the boat rose to devour the king like wolves and the waves at the stern of the boat were attacking Enki like a lion.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: J.A. Black, G. Cunningham, E. Fluckiger-Hawker, E. Robson and G. Zólyomi, The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford, 1998-2006), 1.8.1.4, lines 1-26. Text from <a href="https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/c1814.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (ETCSL)</a>; licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, for non-commercial use with credit.</p><p>English: J.A. Black, G. Cunningham, E. Fluckiger-Hawker, E. Robson and G. Zólyomi, The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (Oxford, 1998-2006), 1.8.1.4, translation of lines 1-26. Text from <a href="https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1814.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature (ETCSL)</a>; licensed CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, for non-commercial use with credit.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The Sumerian is the opening of the poem Gilgamesh, Enkidu and the Netherworld, lines 1-26, as the Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature gives it in its composite text, pieced together from tablets from Nippur, Ur and elsewhere. Its first line, &quot;ud re-a ud su₃-ra₂ re-a&quot;, is the opening Peter Pringle sings.</li><li>Sign values carry their index numbers (su₃, ra₂), and a sign written before a word to say what kind of thing it is stands in braces: {d} before a god's name, {ĝiš} before things of wood. ETCSL's spelling is set in the usual signs (š, ĝ, ḫ); the words are unchanged.</li><li>The English is ETCSL's translation, which renders lines 1-26 as one passage; it is set here in lines at its semicolons and full stops. A question mark in brackets is ETCSL's own, where the meaning is uncertain.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="QUcTsFe1PVs" data-title="Epic of Gilgamesh: sung in Sumerian from the opening of Gilgamesh, Enkidu and the Netherworld (ud re-a ud su3-ra2 re-a), with a three-string long-necked lute; Pringle's own reconstruction"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">sung in Sumerian from the opening of Gilgamesh, Enkidu and the Netherworld (ud re-a ud su3-ra2 re-a), with a three-string long-necked lute; Pringle's own reconstruction</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Peter Pringle. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QUcTsFe1PVs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Iliad and Odyssey</span><span class="sg-chain-h">many scholars see its themes, episodes and verses echoed in both Homeric epics</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Alexander Romance</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Alexander's quest for the water of life was inspired by Gilgamesh's quest for eternal youth</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Princess Mononoke</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1997</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Hayao Miyazaki's film is partly based on the Cedar Forest episode</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gilgamesh, Enkidu, and the Netherworld</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Sumerian poem translated into Akkadian as the twelfth tablet</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gilgamesh and Huwawa</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Sumerian poem behind the Cedar Forest episode</span></li></ul></section>
